---

## 4. Anti-Patterns

- ❌ **Color-only encoding.** Never rely on green/amber/red alone — the coin **count** + tooltip + `aria-label` duplicate the meaning.
- ❌ **Absolute currency mixing.** Don't compare raw `$` vs `cxj` numbers directly; tiering is **relative within the loaded set**, so it stays meaningful across sources.
- ❌ **Cramming raw `↓0.00015 / ↑0.0006`** into a small grid cell — it truncates and overwhelms. The coin rating is the glanceable layer; the tooltip is the detail layer.
- ❌ **Making the rating interactive.** It's an indicator — `pointer-events-none` + `role="img"` prevent a dead tab-stop and click-trap.

---

## 5. Accessibility Checklist

- [x] `<span role="img">` with `aria-label` exposes cost tier + estimate to screen readers.
- [x] Decorative icons marked `aria-hidden="true"`.
- [x] Meaning conveyed by **count + color + text**, not color alone (WCAG 1.4.1).
- [x] `text-success` (green) / `text-warning` (amber) / `text-error` (red) on `bg-base-100` neutral surfaces meet AA at icon scale when paired with the count encoding.
- [x] Indicator is non-interactive (`pointer-events-none`) → no phantom focus target.
- [x] Native `title` provides a hover/焦点 tooltip with full units and source.

---

## 6. Implementation Steps

1. Replace `src/components/chat/ChatLLMModelSelector.vue` with the code above.
2. Confirm the tier split feels right for your catalogue — tweak the two ratios (`0.33` / `0.66`) in `getPriceTier` if "moderate" is over/under-populated.
3. If you prefer a different request profile, edit the single constant: `const STANDARD_REQUEST = { input: 1000, output: 1000 }` (e.g. `{ input: 2000, output: 500 }` for context-heavy chat).
4. Open the selector, hover a card → verify the tooltip shows both raw rates and the estimated per-request cost.
5. Sanity check relative order: Haiku / DeepSeek should show **1 green coin**, a frontier model **3 red coins**.
6. Run Lighthouse (Accessibility ≥ 90) and Tab through the selector to confirm the rating adds no focus stop.

## 1. Direct Recommendation

I built **3 standalone HTML mockups** of the model selector, all sharing the same data model: cost = `input_per_1K + output_per_1K` for a **standard request (1K in + 1K out)**, bucketed into a **3-coin DaisyUI rating** (green = cheap → red = expensive). Each explores a different density/personality for the card. Pick one and I'll port it into `ChatLLMModelSelector.vue`.

> **Note on tiering:** linear min/max bucketing breaks with outliers (Opus at $0.09 squashes everything else to "cheap"). These mockups use **log-scale bucketing**, which keeps Haiku/DeepSeek genuinely cheap and frontier models genuinely expensive.

---

## 2. Variant Comparison

| Variant | Layout | Density | Best for |
|---|---|---|---|
| **V1 — Grid Cards** | 2-col responsive grid | Medium | Default popover, touch-friendly |
| **V2 — Dense List** | Full-width rows | High | Many models, desktop power users |
| **V3 — Rich Cards** | Vertical cards + cost meter | Low | Explaining *why* a model is expensive |

All three: coins never convey meaning by color alone (count + `aria-label` + `title`).

---

